::STAT_U05_PT_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U05_PT_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.1
Question:
A value calculated from a sample is called what?
{
~A. Parameter#A parameter describes an entire population.
=B. Statistic#A statistic is calculated from sample data.
~C. Population#A population is the full group, not the calculated value.
~D. Treatment#A treatment is assigned in an experiment.
}

::STAT_U05_PT_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U05_PT_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.2
Question:
Why is random sampling important?
{
~A. It guarantees no variation#Random samples still vary.
~B. It replaces the need for a population#The population must still be defined.
~C. It always proves causation#Causation depends on study design, not random sampling alone.
=D. It helps the sample represent the population#Random sampling reduces selection bias and supports representativeness.
}

::STAT_U05_PT_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U05_PT_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.3
Question:
A sample chosen because people are easy to reach is usually what type?
{
~A. Cluster sample#Cluster samples choose whole groups.
~B. Stratified sample#Stratified samples choose from subgroups.
=C. Convenience sample#Convenience samples use easily available participants.
~D. Census#A census includes everyone.
}

::STAT_U05_PT_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U05_PT_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.1
Question:
A survey estimate is 62% with margin of error 4 percentage points. What interval is represented?
{
=A. 58% to 66%#Subtract and add 4 percentage points.
~B. 54% to 58%#This interval is not centered at 62%.
~C. 62% to 66%#This only adds the margin.
~D. 4% to 62%#The margin is not the lower endpoint.
}

::STAT_U05_PT_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U05_PT_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.2
Question:
What does confidence level describe?
{
~A. The number of people surveyed#Sample size is not the definition of confidence level.
=B. The long-run success rate of the interval method#Confidence level describes the method over repeated samples.
~C. The exact percent who agree#A sample percent is an estimate, not confidence level.
~D. The graph title#A graph title does not define confidence.
}

::STAT_U05_PT_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U05_PT_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.3
Question:
What do repeated simulated samples show?
{
~A. That every estimate is identical#Repeated samples usually differ.
~B. That no model is needed#Simulation is based on a model or process.
~C. That sampling cannot be studied#Sampling variability can be studied through simulation.
=D. How estimates vary#Simulation helps display sampling variability.
}

::STAT_U05_PT_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U05_PT_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.4
Question:
An observed result near the center of simulated results is usually what?
{
~A. Proof of bias#A typical result alone does not prove bias.
=B. Consistent with the model#A central result is typical under the model.
~C. Impossible under the model#A typical result is not impossible.
~D. A census#A census means data from everyone.
}

::STAT_U05_PT_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U05_PT_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.4; MLA.STAT.DCR.5
Question:
Which design assigns treatments?
{
=A. Experiment#Experiments assign treatments to compare effects.
~B. Convenience sample#Convenience sampling describes how people are selected.
~C. Survey#Surveys ask questions.
~D. Observational study#Observational studies do not assign treatment.
}

::STAT_U05_PT_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U05_PT_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.6; MLA.STAT.INF.7
Question:
In a randomized experiment, a simulated model shows differences from -2 to 2 are common, but the observed treatment difference is 8. What is the best conclusion?
{
~A. The treatments must be identical#The observed difference is far from typical simulated differences, so identical treatment effects are not supported.
~B. The study is only a convenience survey#The situation describes a randomized experiment, not a convenience survey.
=C. The treatment difference is unusual and may be statistically meaningful#Random assignment supports treatment comparison, and the simulation shows the observed difference is unusual.
~D. The difference should be ignored because all differences are random#Simulation helps decide whether a difference is typical or unusual; this one is unusual.
}

::STAT_U05_PT_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U05_PT_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.5; MLA.STAT.DMC.1
Question:
A report compares two random samples from two populations and claims one population has a higher mean. What should you check first?
{
=A. Whether the samples were random and the conclusion matches the data#Random samples support population inference, and report evaluation checks whether the claim follows from the evidence.
~B. Whether the headline is exciting#A headline does not determine whether the inference is valid.
~C. Whether the graph uses the brightest color#Color choice does not determine whether the statistical claim is valid.
~D. Whether the report avoids all statistics#A report comparing populations should use appropriate statistics, not avoid them.
}
